#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf02a3a-6507-5e24-9dfe-fdc65427917b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478718} {:answer/id #uuid "cb480f73-b28d-5d1d-bf28-114396c6f5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478886} {:answer/id #uuid "d40c479d-3512-5ad6-901c-ae8cc9ca656d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478896} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9ed06d-7edb-5dc7-a0b5-315d2f4f833d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478932} {:answer/id #uuid "f4006751-8828-5338-b289-001c5c562f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d40c479d-3512-5ad6-901c-ae8cc9ca656d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478896} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c55d75d-2389-5139-9713-9b0001a3628b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "824e717c-810d-5647-9a31-c9225776fabe" :question/math-academy-id "q-165280" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2y-xy,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6645f2-694d-5701-b91f-bf076971ef68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478601} {:answer/id #uuid "4d211281-45d7-5aac-97a5-b16e835059d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478667} {:answer/id #uuid "53caf8ff-2acc-51ae-aaa5-97dfb713b748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478678} {:answer/id #uuid "64d89caa-06b3-5a99-b92b-722d4926b374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478705} {:answer/id #uuid "dd87415c-114c-567c-b99f-b5fa2701b9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b6645f2-694d-5701-b91f-bf076971ef68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478601} :answer-field/id #uuid "549b5d38-f4aa-575f-b0f7-90b5dcfff3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d222dcc8-f398-5562-bff7-74444e587da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-165281" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a74912-9939-5160-b2f2-fc9f75995b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478590} {:answer/id #uuid "489fb4e2-401a-51bd-8367-54c21281a25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478661} {:answer/id #uuid "af934f8e-6b47-50f3-976d-130962164ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} {:answer/id #uuid "d704c90e-1c68-5f35-9050-f1ea9764f3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d81125-ee3b-5e23-ba6b-3f5a3fc654e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af934f8e-6b47-50f3-976d-130962164ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478844} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f83ac4-379e-5a13-8c4c-6ea313e7d773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4437c604-b7f0-5633-b7e5-9971f03bd2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-165283" :question/problem "Given that $v(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =(2r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0466a8b0-0e72-50a8-9e40-0c1214a8a281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478566} {:answer/id #uuid "074a0854-45c8-5e8e-88a6-4963ee2622c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478571} {:answer/id #uuid "1a085611-6858-54c9-855d-19621bc6a77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478598} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1073de-8640-5caf-b844-a33c80d8ae74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478837} {:answer/id #uuid "d4935e49-5ceb-5de5-a639-1d44e14dc969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0466a8b0-0e72-50a8-9e40-0c1214a8a281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478566} :answer-field/id #uuid "52931c2c-52e1-5448-b25c-5e4f502aa192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7396bdb2-9333-5e99-8459-ac9d52a00034" :question/math-academy-id "q-165285" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a66db9c-0ec7-5bff-93d3-e61113901baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478640} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6ab715-8c58-5119-aca6-445e2d5a78d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478641} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacd52a-7451-5471-aa59-d1a60f4350e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478688} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfdc48d-b632-5519-8ba3-19a0e2f15c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478744} {:answer/id #uuid "813ed5fe-e565-55ae-b0a3-dcc8c9821dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a66db9c-0ec7-5bff-93d3-e61113901baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f8e063-53b4-59b1-a70a-2cbd7cdb3bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c985905c-7d24-536e-9cc0-8e7d7e1b4d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-37186" :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d87c61-ce4c-51b4-a48d-e8a1bd1e34a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842} {:answer/id #uuid "0f53b13e-3023-5aa6-80ef-b86a7b9a981e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478579} {:answer/id #uuid "19655a9a-cd78-5629-b63c-3d39429c6795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478596} {:answer/id #uuid "a74e8615-6e66-546e-bfc7-29b7cec5ca41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} :answer-field/id #uuid "086dfdbb-92b2-5cae-9238-3f1a3c6feab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-37196" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r} & =(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{1}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a5455a6-a620-598c-ad7d-fe4df9304006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478739} {:answer/id #uuid "be832724-0d5c-5c12-8cac-57e9d7fe56e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478866} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e0ff91-463d-5009-a863-3db111e986e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478879} {:answer/id #uuid "cae67d8e-975c-57a3-acb6-aea452effb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478883} {:answer/id #uuid "ed302be1-425e-5d43-9c40-6e14e1503e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e0ff91-463d-5009-a863-3db111e986e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478879} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b6129c-b733-5c71-92b5-462c14e827e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5abfa64-ddd6-5ad1-8a8c-38f9b879752f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95001" :question/problem "Given that $v(r,\\theta )=r(2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =(2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f1b62c-f9ad-5062-b0e1-04d51f193f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{3}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478564} {:answer/id #uuid "1506c3d3-af67-5f60-9fbf-f85df4c42324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478586} {:answer/id #uuid "61401e41-69fe-5937-b2e0-2a0aea6f6035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478699} {:answer/id #uuid "b9805fe1-fc17-516a-93d3-42522d950906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478860} {:answer/id #uuid "d46accdd-6789-5230-a789-5a2c14364ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1506c3d3-af67-5f60-9fbf-f85df4c42324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478586} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbfbb460-7f02-5f92-88f0-57dd4c8eadf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a13109-9c88-5e83-9206-805f97530d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-95045" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{3}+{z}^{4},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +3{r}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abaecde-f0c9-53d9-a58e-fb98f60eda2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478600} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6054fc-1437-5b2d-8432-34493b15c6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478608} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8cc96a-5c3e-522a-bb6f-d8ebe3a3b80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478691} {:answer/id #uuid "860111b7-3db7-5aa8-8df9-cd797e251089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478768} {:answer/id #uuid "982a579f-34d1-56d7-a91d-7ee313227e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d8cc96a-5c3e-522a-bb6f-d8ebe3a3b80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478691} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b299f98-e7d5-5d6a-b095-d0d004a31211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10ac6b1a-97da-521c-a5c2-16923318bd3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95108" :question/problem "Given that $p(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $p(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂p}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂p}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂p}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂p}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $n$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂p}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂x} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂p}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂p}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6d8b25-1be0-507f-9e45-600b11128f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478574} {:answer/id #uuid "194c2f16-5d03-56da-8f8a-2451146b01a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478595} {:answer/id #uuid "27127698-eb1f-5556-ba20-69f2d7bce69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478617} {:answer/id #uuid "80a52cdb-4432-54c2-aba3-ba66914bcbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478752} {:answer/id #uuid "91e129af-e354-55a9-b266-adc2bb6d4a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "194c2f16-5d03-56da-8f8a-2451146b01a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478595} :answer-field/id #uuid "74be8c02-d536-5d47-b824-c1806a52210c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5298f54-bfaf-5a4f-ac68-b78b607bf15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95115" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8d5bbd-e2b1-533d-9bdd-c6ed6c668040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478606} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2092ad-3f11-5666-b765-95bb966954f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478716} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9972e3-8762-5cb3-b03e-679f5020939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478720} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa2b02-1db0-5fce-a974-ddf0773d035d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478779} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3c903d-f537-5005-92b3-162fe2194e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa3c903d-f537-5005-92b3-162fe2194e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478838} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d7bd4f-ecaa-56a0-8783-ed8687ea01ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41bc3280-6f3c-5ef7-84dc-f2df8938f974" :question/math-academy-id "q-95135" :question/problem "Given that $v(r,\\theta )=r(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ),$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂v}{∂y}=\\frac{∂v}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂r}=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d25be9-c593-5605-a401-5d2bd749db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478585} {:answer/id #uuid "7368bb2b-738b-5553-9b24-d72d10f86673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478728} {:answer/id #uuid "84dcc6f3-d690-5f72-8730-48a6a222d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478763} {:answer/id #uuid "952ab814-3b74-5511-bb1b-17a92c942267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478803} {:answer/id #uuid "e73b53e8-31b8-587a-95b0-e9d5813a7d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7368bb2b-738b-5553-9b24-d72d10f86673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478728} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bff08b3-d2ad-542d-9f8a-a1ea880775f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d1b83b-2766-5063-936f-a018502be867" :question/math-academy-id "q-95137" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "893956da-d416-51f2-95f7-dc12fb1c1725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478773} {:answer/id #uuid "9713ade3-5604-530d-b802-1fdfc640a4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478807} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c08b7-43fb-5995-8666-a7c19ff8a830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478854} {:answer/id #uuid "daa43c07-cab4-59d9-96d9-b09db9596eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478903} {:answer/id #uuid "f69418f1-6362-5b4e-8489-03eacbf8b9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9713ade3-5604-530d-b802-1fdfc640a4e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478807} :answer-field/id #uuid "45082c16-ebec-5599-aae3-e180ec356a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e54cb552-8c05-5b9c-84f1-978bbe7cbf63" :question/math-academy-id "q-95168" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂g}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}={x}^{2}+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188706c6-3a9a-59e2-a321-b5f7df7e0d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478593} {:answer/id #uuid "48daba33-40ff-5944-964d-6c1da24ee2b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478662} {:answer/id #uuid "b483533f-bf6d-5af3-85eb-651c6a343298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "rz{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478851} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd84997-1bc6-5e77-af1c-2104ef3b0bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478869} {:answer/id #uuid "cb23270c-5341-5bc3-a497-83e69396e2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "188706c6-3a9a-59e2-a321-b5f7df7e0d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478593} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5f4636-c6b9-5bae-8b55-2a495ce5b39c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb779ef9-c9f2-5338-9ff6-677718e082ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-95169" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂h}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂h}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta } & =-(rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578fd67e-6bee-5c2b-b8bf-bcc27293593f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478681} {:answer/id #uuid "67ac1cbd-e9a7-5e4f-a6c5-90683547e24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478712} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6caf2a-21be-526b-986f-2f7d216426b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478839} {:answer/id #uuid "b97ad485-d546-55df-a46d-f931c8749f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478859} {:answer/id #uuid "f4265219-f53f-5148-b2f2-d73b22c820e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{z}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa6caf2a-21be-526b-986f-2f7d216426b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478839} :answer-field/id #uuid "33abe709-ff3f-57d7-b112-d74c4aa40f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5265619-0ee7-5042-83d2-70d3e989522b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95170" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=x{z}^{4}+y,$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}={z}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={z}^{4}, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =({z}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={z}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
